@begin(section)

@title(Contributing)

@i(Thank you so much for considering contribution to dsolv!)

First, please read the dsolv @c(Code of Conduct). This project will not take
any contribution coming from those who do not abide by the code of conduct.
This means that if a person is currently under disciplinary action via
avenues set forth in that document, we will ignore your PR and/or any issues
you may log.

A contribution can be large or small, code or non-code. To make a
contribution, first log a GitHub issue. Talk about what you want, and ask for
other's opinions. The project tracks its work with
@link[uri="https://github.com/beads/beads"](Beads) issues in the repository.

When you go to make the PR, please use the following checklist to test
whether or not it is likely to be accepted:

@begin(enum)
@item(@b(Is it based on the @c(main) branch?)) Please make PRs to the
@c(main) branch.
@item(@b(Do you have tests in your PR, and do they pass?)) Tests are in two
places in dsolv: the @c(tests/) directory, where more or less normal unit
tests (using @link[uri="https://shinmera.github.io/parachute/"](Parachute))
reside; and the @c(test/resources/scripts) directory, where scripty
integration tests reside. You must have at least a script test (and
preferably one or more unit tests) as a "spot-check" of your feature if the
PR is to be merged. The test need not be elaborate; a simple test is better
than no tests.
@item(@b(Is your PR backwards compatible?)) The biggest feature dsolv
provides is backwards compatibility.

We only consider backwards incompatible changes in the form of new options
for the "correct" behavior and switching the default for that option in a new
version, ideally a major version.

If dsolv breaks a build, it is a bug. If it breaks a build in a way that
can't be fixed by configuration, it is a bad bug.

A good test if a PR is "backwards compatible" is if it changes any previously
merged script test and if it breaks any of them.
@item(@b(Did you add documentation around the feature in your PR?)) Generally
this at least means adding something to the @c(Command Reference) section of
this manual (see the docs-writing instructions in the repository's
@c(.agents/skills/) directory).
@item(@b(Did you add an entry to the Changelog?)) This project keeps a
curated changelog in @c(CHANGELOG.md).
@end(enum)

There are some exceptions to the above rules. For example, if your patch is
less than two lines' difference from the previous version, your PR may be a
"typo" PR, which may qualify to get around some of the above rules. Just ask
the team on your GitHub issue.

@end(section)
